ID: Ch6-Intro
TEXT:

"All right, I call that a plan," Daren says, clapping dirt off his gauntlets as our council breaks up.

We sit outside the cave mouth under a bleached noon sky. Without Kate's magic the clearing would bake like a kiln; as it is, frost feathers the grass around her boots, keeping the heat at a manageable simmer. Three days ago, she would have sat twenty paces apart with her spine to a tree and one eye on our exit routes. Now the frost ring extends around all four of us. She has not moved closer—we have simply stopped being excluded from her perimeter.

"Noon is on us," Daren says, buckling his sword belt. "We move now. No travel after sundown in this territory, and we have miles to cover before nightfall. Rose, do you have every root you set out for?"

Rose blinks, her hand dropping to an empty hip. "The satchel. I dropped it in the gully when the goblins broke cover."

"Goblins take meat and metal, not dried leaf," Daren says, offering a hand to pull her up. "It will be right where you left it. Come on, I'll walk you back."

I move to follow, but fingers clamp around my linen sleeve. Kate.

"Hold on," she says under her breath.

Her shoulders are rigid. Her jaw works twice before sound comes out.

"Were you..."

She stops. Looks past my shoulder, then directly into my eyes.

"Were you really smitten with me since you first saw me?"

Of every sentence I prepared for between dawn and sundown, this one sits firmly at the bottom of the ledger. Thank you, Daren. What am I supposed to give her now?

choice(""Maybe a little."", Ch6-Maybe, v_current_scene = Ch6-Maybe, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)
choice(""Of course not. Daren was just joking earlier. Don't worry about it."", Ch6-Joking, v_current_scene = Ch6-Joking, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch6-Maybe
TEXT:

"Maybe a little," I tell her.

"You were?" The frost around her heels spreads two inches into the dirt. "I thought he was running his mouth. Look—I'm not trying to be cruel, but no. Not now. Not while we're in this."

"I said a little. What brought this on? You don't usually fret over tavern gossip."

Kate turns her head toward the tree line, drawing in a dry breath.

"When those three mages attacked us this morning, something settled in my head. You and Rose are the only people I've met on this island who don't look at me like an abomination. Even when strangers keep their mouths shut, their eyes give them away. They see a monster. Daren is the same. Whatever pretty words you fed me, he doesn't believe I'm human. He's waiting for the horns to sprout, and the day will come when I have to cross steel with him just like the rest."

She is already picking apart the lie I told to keep her from bolting. I wanted to shore up her faith in Daren; turns out there was nothing there to shore up.

She brings her gaze back, hard and searching.

"But you and Rose... you're different. You don't flinch. There's no fear in your eyes, no hand creeping toward a hilt. You talk to me like I'm a person on the road. I don't know what's broken in your head to make you like that, but... thank you. For letting me stand in a circle with people and not feel like a caged dog."

"Found it!" Daren's voice booms through the pines before he steps into the clearing, Rose beside him, the canvas satchel slung over his shoulder. "Tangled in a briar patch, dry and intact. We're clear to march."

He slows as he takes in the two of us standing chest to chest in the frost.

"I'm sorry, did we interrupt something?"

choice(""Yeah, Kate was just about to profess her love for me."", Ch6-Profess, v_current_scene = Ch6-Profess)
choice(""No, you're right, we should get going."", Ch6-Going, v_current_scene = Ch6-Going)


ID: Ch6-Joking
TEXT:

"Of course not. Daren was just joking earlier. Don't worry about it."

Kate's shoulders drop an inch. A sharp puff of air leaves her nose.

"Good," she says. "That's what I needed to hear."

"What brought this on? You don't usually fret over tavern gossip."

Kate turns her head toward the tree line, drawing in a dry breath.

"When those three mages attacked us this morning, something settled in my head. You and Rose are the only people I've met on this island who don't look at me like an abomination. Even when strangers keep their mouths shut, their eyes give them away. They see a monster. Daren is the same. Whatever pretty words you fed me, he doesn't believe I'm human. He's waiting for the horns to sprout, and the day will come when I have to cross steel with him just like the rest."

She is already picking apart the lie I told to keep her from bolting. I wanted to shore up her faith in Daren; turns out there was nothing there to shore up.

She brings her gaze back, hard and searching.

"But you and Rose... you're different. You don't flinch. There's no fear in your eyes, no hand creeping toward a hilt. You talk to me like I'm a person on the road. I don't know what's broken in your head to make you like that, but... thank you. For letting me stand in a circle with people and not feel like a caged dog."

"Found it!" Daren's voice booms through the pines before he steps into the clearing, Rose beside him, the canvas satchel slung over his shoulder. "Tangled in a briar patch, dry and intact. We're clear to march."

He slows as he takes in the two of us standing chest to chest in the frost.

"I'm sorry, did we interrupt something?"

choice(""Yeah, Kate was just about to profess her love for me."", Ch6-Profess, v_current_scene = Ch6-Profess)
choice(""No, you're right, we should get going."", Ch6-Going, v_current_scene = Ch6-Going)


ID: Ch6-Profess
TEXT:

"Yeah, Kate was just about to profess her love for me."

Air hisses between her teeth. In a heartbeat, an icicle long as a broadsword condenses in her grip, its needle point resting dead against my Adam's apple.

"Say that again. I dare you."

"There she is," I wheeze, raising both palms. "Back to normal. Daren's right, though—sun's climbing and we have ground to eat. Let's get going."

The ice blade dissolves into vapor. Daren gives me a look that questions my sanity, and we turn toward the cave mouth to make sure we leave nothing behind.

choice("Continue", Ch6-Packing, v_current_scene = Ch6-Packing)


ID: Ch6-Going
TEXT:

"No, you're right, we should get going."

Daren studies the space between us for three seconds too long, clearly weighing five different questions, then decides the road matters more. We head inside the cave to make sure we leave nothing behind.

choice("Continue", Ch6-Packing, v_current_scene = Ch6-Packing)


ID: Ch6-Packing
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_treasure == 0 && v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1) {
Before hauling out, I inventory the morning's salvage: a light crossbow stripped from the ambushers and steel daggers lifted off Dave's crew.

We regroup with Rose and take the south trail.

We haven't cleared a quarter mile before Daren falls in beside me, eyeing the weapon lashed to my frame.

"Barry. Why is that crossbow riding the outside of your rucksack?"

"Because the canvas is packed solid to the drawstring."

Daren stops mid-stride. "Packed? You're carrying an ordinary cloth sack?"

"Enchanted gear doesn't come cheap if you can't weave a spell to hold it together."

"You have a craftsman walking three paces behind you," Daren says, shaking his head. "Hand it over. It takes five minutes."

He takes the straps, closes his eyes, and drops into the trance. His irises flare amber; a dull, warm luminescence seeps through the canvas weave. Item enchanting is his trade, a detail I managed to forget during the morning's bloodletting. An enchanted pack is worth a king's ransom on the trail: near-limitless volume, zero drag, and items can only be retrieved by someone who holds a clear mental image of what they're reaching for.

Rose steps closer, watching the amber glow in Daren's eyes with frank curiosity.

"All right," Daren says, blinking the magic away. "Do you want it sealed to your touch, or standard? If I lock the weave, nobody extracts an item but you—not even if they know the inventory. Catch is, you can't grant access to anyone else later. Standard is usually enough since thieves can't fish out what they don't know exists, but caution never killed a traveler."

}
#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1 && v_ch3_treasure != 0) {
Before hauling out, I inventory the morning's salvage: a light crossbow stripped from the ambushers, steel daggers lifted off Dave's crew, and the velvet purse of coin taken off the duke of Bagelberry.

We regroup with Rose and take the south trail.

We haven't cleared a quarter mile before Daren falls in beside me, eyeing the weapon lashed to my frame.

"Barry. Why is that crossbow riding the outside of your rucksack?"

"Because the canvas is packed solid to the drawstring."

Daren stops mid-stride. "Packed? You're carrying an ordinary cloth sack?"

"Enchanted gear doesn't come cheap if you can't weave a spell to hold it together."

"You have a craftsman walking three paces behind you," Daren says, shaking his head. "Hand it over. It takes five minutes."

He takes the straps, closes his eyes, and drops into the trance. His irises flare amber; a dull, warm luminescence seeps through the canvas weave. Item enchanting is his trade, a detail I managed to forget during the morning's bloodletting. An enchanted pack is worth a king's ransom on the trail: near-limitless volume, zero drag, and items can only be retrieved by someone who holds a clear mental image of what they're reaching for.

Rose steps closer, watching the amber glow in Daren's eyes with frank curiosity.

"All right," Daren says, blinking the magic away. "Do you want it sealed to your touch, or standard? If I lock the weave, nobody extracts an item but you—not even if they know the inventory. Catch is, you can't grant access to anyone else later. Standard is usually enough since thieves can't fish out what they don't know exists, but caution never killed a traveler."

}
#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1 && v_ch3_treasure == 0) {
We reach the cave entrance to the sound of hooves clattering over stone. Molan, the young fawn, bounces on all four legs, ears pinned forward.

"Mother! Mother, hurry! It's Aunt Rose! She came back!"

The color drains from Rose's face. The easy warmth she wore five minutes ago vanishes, replaced by a hollow, downcast stillness.

Elaria emerges from the shaded rock. "Rose? Is that truly you?"

Rose answers in the low, melodic cadence of the deer tongue, her eyes fixed firmly on the grass. "It has been too long."

"Months," Elaria says gently. "You used to walk these paths every week. You know our hearth is always open to you, Rose. Nothing changes that."

"I know," Rose whispers, her voice catching. "I... we have to move south. The town needs these roots."

"Aunt Rose, you're leaving already?" the fawn asks, trotting forward.

Elaria lowers her muzzle to the fawn's neck. "Patience, little one. She has wounded people waiting on her skill. She will stay longer when she is ready."

Rose gives a stiff, formal bow, her jaw set tight against trembling. "Thank you. And forgive me."

She turns away before either deer can answer. Elaria passes us our packs, adding wrapped bundles of dried fruit and flatbread. "For the road. Roots and wild greens wear thin after three days."

"Thank you for the shelter," I say. "We'll cross paths again."

"Varathia is smaller than mages think," Elaria says.

Kate and I offer our farewells, shoulder our gear, and secure the morning's salvage: the ambusher's crossbow and daggers from Dave's crew.

We catch up to Rose, who walks three paces ahead, staring fixedly at the trail with her arms crossed over her chest. I wonder if I should break the silence or let her carry it alone.

}
