ID: Ch6-Met
TEXT:

"Hey, I've been meaning to ask, how did you and Elaria meet? You seemed to know each other quite well."

Rose starts, blinking out of her reverie. She pulls her warm smile back into place, though the tension lingers in her shoulders.

"A few years back," she says, adjusting the strap of her satchel. "I was foraging along the ridge and found Molan burning with fever. I ground some willow-bark salve and steeped a draught for his lungs. We've traded news and dried herbs ever since."

"And then something changed?"

The smile stays, but the light leaves her eyes.

"That obvious? Yes. Though neither Elaria nor I had a hand in it. When our grand priest died months ago, panic took the lower quarters. The food stores shrank to nothing; soon enough, only the merchant quarter could afford conjured grain. People took bows into the woods. The hunts grew bloody, and war between the townsfolk and the beasts followed. Molan's father was killed in the fighting—struck down by a hunter from my own street. After the funeral, I couldn't look Elaria in the eye. That's why the visits stopped."

choice(""It wasn't your fault."", Ch6-Fault, v_current_scene = Ch6-Fault)
choice(""That's stupid. You weren't the one who killed him."", Ch6-Stupid, v_current_scene = Ch6-Stupid, v_ch6_rose_deer = 0)
choice(""Elaria didn't seem to mind your presence."", Ch6-Presence, v_current_scene = Ch6-Presence, v_ch6_rose_deer = 1)


ID: Ch6-Happened
TEXT:

"Hey, I've been meaning to ask, did something happen between you and Elaria?"

Rose starts, blinking out of her reverie. She tries for her usual easy smile, but the corners of her mouth tighten and her eyes stay clouded.

"That obvious? Yes. Though neither Elaria nor I had a hand in it. When our grand priest died months ago, panic took the lower quarters. The food stores shrank to nothing; soon enough, only the merchant quarter could afford conjured grain. People took bows into the woods. The hunts grew bloody, and war between the townsfolk and the beasts followed. Molan's father was killed in the fighting—struck down by a hunter from my own street. After the funeral, I couldn't look Elaria in the eye. That's why the visits stopped."

choice(""It wasn't your fault."", Ch6-Fault, v_current_scene = Ch6-Fault)
choice(""That's stupid. You weren't the one who killed him."", Ch6-Stupid, v_current_scene = Ch6-Stupid, v_ch6_rose_deer = 0)
choice(""Elaria didn't seem to mind your presence."", Ch6-Presence, v_current_scene = Ch6-Presence, v_ch6_rose_deer = 1)


ID: Ch6-Fault
TEXT:

"It wasn't your fault."

"I know that here," Rose says, touching her temple. "There was nothing I could have done to turn that arrow. I don't even know the man who loosed it—only that he walked the same cobblestones I do. But knowing doesn't stop the rot in my chest."

choice(""You're only making things worse by deciding to not visit them."", Ch6-Worse, v_current_scene = Ch6-Worse, v_ch6_rose_deer = 1)
choice(""Maybe it would indeed be better to keep your distance for now."", Ch6-Better, v_current_scene = Ch6-Better, v_ch6_rose_deer = 0)


ID: Ch6-Stupid
TEXT:

"That's stupid. You weren't the one who killed him."

Rose lets out a breathless laugh, startled into it.

"Comforting speeches aren't your strong suit, are they, Barry?"

"Never claimed they were. But you laughed, so the medicine worked."

"You're terrible," she says, her smile turning genuine. "Come on. Daren's pulling ahead."

We haven't cleared a quarter mile before Daren falls in beside me, eyeing the weapon lashed to my frame.

"Barry. Why is that crossbow riding the outside of your rucksack?"

"Because the canvas is packed solid to the drawstring."

Daren stops mid-stride. "Packed? You're carrying an ordinary cloth sack?"

"Enchanted gear doesn't come cheap if you can't weave a spell to hold it together."

"You have a craftsman walking three paces behind you," Daren says, shaking his head. "Hand it over. It takes five minutes."

He takes the straps, closes his eyes, and drops into the trance. His irises flare amber; a dull, warm luminescence seeps through the canvas weave. Item enchanting is his trade, a detail I managed to forget during the morning's bloodletting. An enchanted pack is worth a king's ransom on the trail: near-limitless volume, zero drag, and items can only be retrieved by someone who holds a clear mental image of what they're reaching for.

Rose steps closer, watching the amber glow in Daren's eyes with frank curiosity.

"All right," Daren says, blinking the magic away. "Do you want it sealed to your touch, or standard? If I lock the weave, nobody extracts an item but you—not even if they know the inventory. Catch is, you can't grant access to anyone else later. Standard is usually enough since thieves can't fish out what they don't know exists, but caution never killed a traveler."

choice("Secure the backpack", Ch6-Secure, v_current_scene = Ch6-Secure)
choice("Do not secure the backpack", Ch6-Enough, v_current_scene = Ch6-Enough)


ID: Ch6-Presence
TEXT:

"Elaria didn't seem to mind your presence. Personally, I think you're only making things worse by deciding to not visit them."

Rose slows her pace, staring at the dust between her boots. "I... you're right. Seeing Molan run toward me like that... cutting them off was about my own shame, not their grief. They needed a friend, and I hid in town. Next time I walk that ridge, I won't turn away. Thank you, Barry."

Her warmth returns, unforced and clear.

"Any time," I say.

Up ahead, Kate glances over her shoulder. Her mouth quirks into the faintest shadow of a smile before she faces front again.

We haven't cleared a quarter mile before Daren falls in beside me, eyeing the weapon lashed to my frame.

