choice("Secure the backpack", Ch6-Secure, v_current_scene = Ch6-Secure)
choice("Do not secure the backpack", Ch6-Enough, v_current_scene = Ch6-Enough)


ID: Ch6-Secure
TEXT:

set(v_ch6_secure,1)
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 1 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0 || v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 1) {
"Secure it. Better to be safe than sorry."

"As you wish."

Once he finishes weaving the threads around my pack, he passes it back and turns to Rose with an open grin.

"I saw you studying the weave. Would you like yours treated the same?"

Rose's cheeks color. "I would be honored!"

He weaves the enchantment into her satchel in under a minute, and we resume our march.

We spend the next four hours pushing south on the direct line toward Rose's town. Her usual foraging routes loop wide to skirt known beast dens, but Rose pressed for the most direct path—every hour on the trail is another hour her father spends exposed to a failing quarantine—and Daren agreed without argument, pointing out that our group can handle whatever the brush throws at us.

And Varathia finally decides to look like Varathia. The pines give way to a stretch of trees I have no name for—trunks pale as bone, leaves a deep violet that flashes silver when the wind turns them over. Around midday we skirt a shallow valley where a ring of broken pillars stands in the grass, each carved with a spiral of script, humming faintly as we pass like a struck goblet an hour after the toast. Nothing grows within fifty paces of the perimeter. Rose doesn't even glance over. "The locals leave them alone," she says. "So do we."

Rose works as she walks, stripping dried leaves into pouches, identifying bitter-root clusters, and steering us away from blood-bane briars. At one point the trail narrows between two fallen trunks, and she pauses ahead of me to hold a thorny branch aside with her forearm. I duck through, and for a moment we stand in the tight gap together—her shoulder against my chest, her hair brushing my jaw, the sharp green smell of crushed mint rising from her open satchel. "Sorry," she says, not moving immediately. "Tight squeeze." Then she lets the branch spring back and walks on, and I notice I am still holding my breath. It has been hours since the last ambush—an unnerving quiet, considering how this tournament has been going so far.

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 1 && v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1 || v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 1) {
"Secure it. Better to be safe than sorry."

"As you wish."

Once he finishes weaving the threads around my pack, he passes it back and turns to Rose with an open grin.

"I saw you studying the weave. Would you like yours treated the same?"

Rose's cheeks color. "I would be honored!"

He weaves the enchantment into her satchel in under a minute, and we resume our march.

We spend the next four hours pushing south on the direct line toward Rose's town. Her usual foraging routes loop wide to skirt known beast dens, but Rose pressed for the most direct path—every hour on the trail is another hour her father spends exposed to a failing quarantine—and Daren agreed without argument, pointing out that our group can handle whatever the brush throws at us.

And Varathia finally decides to look like Varathia. The pines give way to a stretch of trees I have no name for—trunks pale as bone, leaves a deep violet that flashes silver when the wind turns them over. Around midday we skirt a shallow valley where a ring of broken pillars stands in the grass, each carved with a spiral of script, humming faintly as we pass like a struck goblet an hour after the toast. Nothing grows within fifty paces of the perimeter. Rose doesn't even glance over. "The locals leave them alone," she says. "So do we."

Rose works as she walks, stripping dried leaves into pouches, identifying bitter-root clusters, and steering us away from blood-bane briars. At one point the trail narrows between two fallen trunks, and she pauses ahead of me to hold a thorny branch aside with her forearm. I duck through, and for a moment we stand in the tight gap together—her shoulder against my chest, her hair brushing my jaw, the sharp green smell of crushed mint rising from her open satchel. "Sorry," she says, not moving immediately. "Tight squeeze." Then she lets the branch spring back and walks on, and I notice I am still holding my breath. It has been hours since the last ambush—an unnerving quiet, considering how this tournament has been going so far.

Kate keeps pace on stubbornness alone. The crossbow wound from this morning has drained the color from her skin, and twice her boot catches on exposed root, nearly sending her into the dirt. When Rose offers an arm and suggests a breather by the brook, Kate shakes her off with a curt hiss through her teeth. "I'm fine. Keep moving."

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 2 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 0) {
"Secure it. Better to be safe than sorry."

"As you wish."

Once he finishes weaving the threads around my pack, he passes it back and turns to Rose with an open grin.

"I saw you studying the weave. Would you like yours treated the same?"

Rose's cheeks color. "I would be honored!"

He weaves the enchantment into her satchel in under a minute, then turns back to me, beaming with sudden realization.

"Barry! It just hit me. Remember what I said about traveling all the way to a city so you could strike a local and let the tournament ward throw you back to the mainland? We don't need a city anymore. We have Rose!"

Cold sweat breaks across the back of my neck. How did I not see this coming the second she joined our circle?

"No," I say, stepping back. "Even to go home, I can't just strike Rose."

"Oh, don't worry about that," Rose says, smiling warmly. "I'd be glad to help! And besides, the ward would catch you before your fist even touched me, just like that mage this morning."

Kate's eyes lock onto me, sharp and searching.

"Right," I stammer. "Only... what about escorting Rose home?"

"Kate and I are more than enough to handle a few monsters," Daren says, watching me closely now. "What's the trouble, Barry?"

"Nothing. Just... I..."

My tongue feels thick. Every excuse that surfaces sounds clumsier than the last.

"I don't..."

Daren's smile dies. The warmth leaves his face, replaced by a dark, deliberate stillness.

"Barry. You didn't come here against your will, did you?"

A breath rattles in my chest. "No... No, I didn't."

"Then what are you doing here?"

"I joined for the same reason as everyone else," I say, the truth dropping out flat. "I'm after the Magium. It's been my dream to become a mage for as long as I can remember."

Daren stares at me, his jaw set hard. "When were you planning on telling me this?"

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 3 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 0) {
"Secure it. Better to be safe than sorry."

"As you wish."

Once he finishes weaving the threads around my pack, he passes it back and turns to Rose with an open grin.

"I saw you studying the weave. Would you like yours treated the same?"

Rose's cheeks color. "I would be honored!"

He weaves the enchantment into her satchel in under a minute, then turns back to me, beaming with sudden realization.

"Barry! It just hit me. Rose is an herbalist! She can help you find the plants you need to cure your sister. She knows every medicinal stalk on this continent."

"I... well, I don't think—"

"Barry, your sister is ill?" Rose asks, stepping closer with immediate concern. "What kind of herbs are you looking for, exactly?"

"I'm... looking for a plant with long leaves, serrated margins, and purple fruit with white spots."

Rose tilts her head. "I know that shrub, but... those aren't medicinal. They're used to steep a mild tea. Are you sure you aren't mistaken?"

Sweat gathers under my collar. Kate is watching me intently.

"Right... now that I think about it, the berries were blue with white spots, not purple."

"Plants with blue spotted fruit have rounded lobes, never long serrated leaves," Rose says gently. "What ailment are you treating? Maybe I can help you compound the remedy."

"It's... a medicine for... uh..."

Words fail me. The lie collapses under its own weight.

"I don't..."

Daren's expression hardens into slate.

"Barry. You didn't come here looking for medicinal herbs, did you?"

"No... No, I didn't."

"Then what are you doing here?"

"I joined for the same reason as everyone else," I say, the words landing like lead. "I'm after the Magium. It's been my dream to become a mage for as long as I can remember."

"And that story about your sister? That was all a lie too?"

"Yes..."

Daren's hands drop to his sides. "When were you planning on telling me this?"

}
choice("Continue", Ch6-Travel, v_current_scene = Ch6-Travel) if (v_ch2_human_reason == 1 || v_ch5_admit_lie == 1)
choice(""I wasn't going to."", Ch6-Wasnt, v_current_scene = Ch6-Wasnt) if (v_ch2_human_reason == 2 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 0 || v_ch2_human_reason == 3 && v_ch5_admit_lie == 0)
