ID: Ch6-Wasnt
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 0) {
"I wasn't going to. I just kind of figured I'd manage to wing it until the end of the tournament."

"I see," Daren says.

He turns on his heel and marches down the path, armor clanking with every stride, not looking back.

Rose watches him go, distressed. "Did I say something I shouldn't have?"

"No," Kate says flatly. "This is entirely Barry's fault. Don't worry about it."

We spend the next four hours pushing south on the direct line toward Rose's town. Her usual foraging routes loop wide to skirt known beast dens, but Rose pressed for the most direct path—every hour on the trail is another hour her father spends exposed to a failing quarantine—and Daren agreed without argument, pointing out that our group can handle whatever the brush throws at us.

And Varathia finally decides to look like Varathia. The pines give way to a stretch of trees I have no name for—trunks pale as bone, leaves a deep violet that flashes silver when the wind turns them over. Around midday we skirt a shallow valley where a ring of broken pillars stands in the grass, each carved with a spiral of script, humming faintly as we pass like a struck goblet an hour after the toast. Nothing grows within fifty paces of the perimeter. Rose doesn't even glance over. "The locals leave them alone," she says. "So do we."

